# 25. Security UX

Security should not make NINJA difficult to use.

Keep the current clean interface.

Show clear messages such as:

```text
Session expired. Please sign in again.
```

instead of technical errors.

For suspicious activity:

```text
Too many requests. Please try again later.
```

Do not expose internal security details to users.

---

# 26. Security Acceptance Criteria

The implementation is complete only when:

* HTTPS is enabled in production.
* Passwords are securely hashed.
* Authentication is protected.
* Authorization is enforced server-side.
* JWT/session security is implemented correctly.
* API endpoints are protected.
* Rate limiting exists for sensitive endpoints.
* CORS is restricted.
* User input is validated and sanitized.
* XSS protections are implemented.
* Security headers are configured.
* Database credentials are private.
* API keys are server-side only.
* `.env` is excluded from Git.
* NINJA commands cannot execute arbitrary system commands.
* Dangerous URLs are rejected.
* Admin APIs are protected.
* Production errors don't expose secrets.
* Security events are logged safely.
* Dependencies are checked for known vulnerabilities.
* No secrets are present in the public repository.
* Existing NINJA functionality continues working.
* Mobile and desktop UI remain functional.

